course: biology
lesson: 203
title: The fish tank: water, gills, heaters and the right neighbours
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A fish tank is a tiny world in a glass box: the fish, the plants, the water and even invisible bacteria all depend on each other, and the owner has to keep it in balance.
explain: Fish breathe the oxygen dissolved in water through their gills, and their droppings make waste that would poison them if friendly bacteria in the filter did not turn it into something safer.
explain: That is why a brand-new tank filled with tap water and stocked with fish the same day often goes wrong: the helpful bacteria have not grown yet. Waiting while they grow is called cycling the tank.
explain: A good owner also checks what each fish needs, warm or cool water, fresh or salt, and how big it will grow, because a fish that fits in another fish's mouth may become its dinner.
fact: New tanks need time for helpful bacteria to grow in the filter and break down fish waste; adding fish to brand-new water can make them ill or kill them, and the waiting time is called cycling.
fact: Fish take oxygen from water through their gills, and a filter, moving water and air bubbles help mix more oxygen into the water.
fact: Tropical fish such as angelfish and neon tetras need warm water kept steady by a heater, while goldfish need cooler water, so they are usually kept apart.
fact: Fish need only as much food as they can eat in a couple of minutes; leftover food rots and makes the water cloudy and dirty.
fact: Fish have no eyelids, so they sleep with their eyes open, resting quietly near the bottom or among plants, and some parrotfish wrap themselves in a bubble of slime to sleep.
fact: Fish that are small enough to fit in another fish's mouth may be eaten, so owners check how big each fish will grow before putting them together.
fact: Betta fish need more room than a tiny bowl, plus warm, filtered water, and male bettas fight each other, so they are kept one male per tank.
fact: A pleco sucking on the glass is scraping off algae (a green slimy growth of tiny plant-like living things) with its sucker mouth, and some kinds of pleco grow much bigger than the shop said.
fact: Fish notice movement and shadows, which often mean a bird is hunting, and they also feel movements in the water with a line of sensors along their sides called the lateral line.
fact: Real clownfish like Nemo live in warm sea water among anemones (soft stinging sea animals that look like flowers), and after the Finding Nemo films many people were reported to buy clownfish they could not care for.
fact: Living plants use up some fish waste, add oxygen in the light and give fish places to hide.
exercise: A new tank is filled with tap water and fish are added the same day. What may happen? | The fish may get ill or die | The fish grow faster | The water becomes cleaner | Nothing, it is the best way
exercise: What is the waiting time for helpful bacteria to grow in a new tank called? | Cycling the tank | Charging the tank | Washing out the tank | Boiling the tank water
exercise: How do fish breathe in a tank? | They take oxygen from water through their gills | They swim up to the top and breathe air with lungs | They do not need oxygen | They breathe through their fins
exercise: What helps mix more oxygen into the tank water? | A filter, moving water and air bubbles | A lid kept tightly shut so no oxygen escapes | Lots of extra food | Turning off all the lights
exercise: An angelfish and a neon tetra swim in a tank. What do they need? | Warm water kept steady with a heater | Ice-cold water | Sea water | No water at all for part of the day, so their fins can dry
exercise: Why are goldfish usually kept apart from tropical fish? | Goldfish need cooler water | Goldfish eat all tropical fish | Tropical fish are scared of gold | Goldfish need salty sea water
exercise: A child pours in lots of fish food. What happens to the leftovers? | They rot and make the water cloudy | The fish save them for later, for when they are hungry | They turn into plants | They float away
exercise: How much food should fish get? | As much as they eat in a couple of minutes | As much as fills the tank right up to the top | One flake a week | As much as a cat eats
exercise: A fish seems awake at night with its eyes open. Why? | Fish have no eyelids and sleep with their eyes open | Fish never sleep at all, not even for a minute | It is waiting for breakfast | Its eyes are stuck open because the water is too cold
exercise: A big fish eats a small fish in the tank. What should owners check? | How big each fish will grow before mixing them | Only the colour of each fish | Whether the fish like each other's names | Nothing, fish never eat fish
exercise: Is a small bowl a good home for a betta fish? | No, it needs more room, warm and filtered water | Yes, bettas love tiny bowls with no filter and cold water | Yes, if the bowl is round | No, bettas need sea water
exercise: Why are male bettas kept one per tank? | Male bettas fight each other | They eat too much | They are shy of each other | They need all the water
exercise: A pleco sucks on the tank glass. What is it doing? | Eating algae | Trying to escape | Kissing its reflection | Breathing through the glass
exercise: A fish swims away when a shadow passes over. Why? | A shadow can mean a hunting bird | Fish are afraid of the dark, like small children | It is chasing the shadow because it thinks it is food | It is playing a game of hide-and-seek
exercise: What is the lateral line? | A line of sensors along a fish's side that feel water movement | The edge of the fish tank, where the glass meets the sand | A fin on top of the fish | The fish's nose
exercise: A clownfish like Nemo is sold in a shop. What kind of water does it need? | Warm sea water | Cold pond water | Plain tap water | Fizzy bottled water
exercise: Why are real plants good in an aquarium? | They use up some waste, add oxygen and give hiding places | They feed on the fish | They make the water warm enough for tropical fish without a heater | They stop fish sleeping
test: «got a tank for my bday, filled it up, chuck the fish in now yeah?» What is right? | Wait while helpful bacteria grow, or the fish may die | Yes, put them all in at once while the water is fresh | Yes, and add extra food too | No, never add fish at all
test: «Nemo's dad lived in the sea, so I'll put a clownfish in my cold tap-water tank» What is right? | Clownfish need warm sea water, so this would kill it | Clownfish love cold tap water | Any fish can live in any water | Clownfish only live in films
test: «the fish never shuts its eyes, is it a zombie lol» What is the truth? | Fish have no eyelids, so they sleep with eyes open | It never sleeps | It is ill | It is watching you all night because it does not trust you
test: What would happen if you fed the fish a whole pot of food every day? | The leftovers would rot and foul the water | The fish would grow huge and healthy | The water would get cleaner | The fish would save it for winter
test: «put the big oscar in with the tiny neons, they'll be mates» What is likely? | The big fish may eat the little ones | They will all be friends | The little ones will gang up together and eat the oscar | They will swap colours
test: «my betta's in a jam jar, he doesn't need more, right?» What is right? | He needs a bigger warm tank with a filter | A jam jar is perfect | He needs ice water | He should share with another male betta
test: In a cartoon two fish chat and breathe bubbles of air out of their mouths. How do real fish breathe? | They take oxygen out of the water through their gills | They breathe the little air bubbles they make with their mouths | They hold their breath all day | They breathe through their tails
test: «that ugly sucker fish is licking the glass, gross» What is it doing? | Cleaning off algae to eat | Trying to get out of the tank | Licking the glass for salt | Talking to the cat outside the glass
test: Count them: a tank has 5 neon tetras and 2 angelfish. How many eyes? | 14 | 7 | 10 | 28
test: Count them: 3 fish and a pet crab with ten legs in the tank. How many legs? | 10 | 8 | 12 | 22
test: «I tapped the glass and they all shot behind the plants» Why? | Fish feel the movement and hide from danger | They wanted the plants to eat | They are deaf so they hid | They were going to sleep
test: «why do they bother with real plants, plastic ones are easier» What do living plants add? | They use up some waste, add oxygen and give hiding places | Nothing at all | They eat the fish | They make the water salty, just like the sea that fish come from
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the necessity of cycling a newly established aquarium? | Time is required for beneficial bacteria to develop which break down the waste of the fish | The water must be boiled before the fish are introduced, so that no living thing remains in it | The fish must be trained to swim in circles | The glass must be allowed to harden
exam: Identify the means by which fish obtain oxygen. | Through the gills, from oxygen dissolved in the water | Through lungs, from air at the surface in all species | Through the fins, from the light | Fish possess no requirement for oxygen
exam: It is given that a large fish and several small fish are proposed to share a single aquarium. Ascertain the principal consideration. | Any fish small enough to fit within the mouth of another may be consumed | Fish of differing colours are invariably hostile | Small fish invariably consume large fish | No consideration is required whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the clownfish represented in the film concerning Nemo. | The species inhabits warm sea water and is unsuited to a cold freshwater tank | The species thrives in cold tap water | The species exists solely in animated films | The species requires no water whatsoever
